SEASONS=[
dict(slug='advent',name='Advent',color='#5b3f8c',colname='Violet (Lutherans often use blue; rose on the 3rd Sunday)',weeks=4,icon='🕯️',
dates='Four Sundays before Christmas. 2026: Sunday 29 November to Thursday 24 December.',
history='''The word comes from the Latin *adventus*, "coming". A season of preparation before Christmas appears in Gaul and Spain in the late 4th and 5th centuries: the Council of Saragossa (380) asked the faithful to be in church daily from 17 December to Epiphany, and Bishop Perpetuus of Tours (d. 490) set a fast from St Martin's Day (11 November), which was nicknamed "St Martin's Lent". Rome kept a shorter season, and Pope Gregory the Great (590–604) is credited with fixing it at four Sundays, the form the West still keeps (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Advent").''',
why='''Advent looks two ways. It prepares for the celebration of Christ's first coming at Bethlehem, and it turns the Church toward his promised return in glory. That is why the first Sunday's Gospel is about the end of the age, the middle Sundays belong to John the Baptist, and the fourth Sunday finally arrives at Mary and the Annunciation. The mood is expectant and hopeful, more like waiting at a window than fasting in the desert.''',
symbols='''**Advent wreath:** an evergreen circle (eternity, life that does not die) with four candles, three violet and one rose, lit one more each Sunday; a white Christ candle is often added at Christmas. The wreath began in Lutheran Germany: Johann Hinrich Wichern lit candles on a cartwheel for the boys of his Hamburg mission school in 1839. **O Antiphons:** seven ancient antiphons sung at Vespers from 17 to 23 December ("O Wisdom", "O Root of Jesse", "O Key of David"…) that became the hymn *O come, O come, Emmanuel*. **Jesse Tree**, **Advent calendar**, **Rorate Masses** by candlelight before dawn.''',
catholic='''Violet vestments, rose on Gaudete Sunday (3rd). The Gloria is omitted at Sunday Mass so that it sounds new at Christmas. Weekday readings follow a fixed pattern: Isaiah's promises through 16 December, then from 17 December the Gospels of Jesus' ancestry and birth. Feasts inside Advent include St Andrew (30 Nov), St Nicholas (6 Dec), the Immaculate Conception (8 Dec, a holy day of obligation in the US), St Juan Diego (9 Dec), Our Lady of Guadalupe (12 Dec) and St Lucy (13 Dec).''',
lutheran='''ELCA congregations mostly use blue (hope) rather than violet (penitence), a practice confirmed in *Evangelical Lutheran Worship* (2006); the LCMS permits both. The Revised Common Lectionary (ELCA) and the LCMS three-year lectionary run close to the Catholic readings. Luther's hymn *Savior of the Nations, Come* (*Nun komm, der Heiden Heiland*, 1524), his translation of St Ambrose's 4th-century *Veni redemptor gentium*, is the classic Lutheran Advent hymn.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia (1913): Advent',NA+'01165a.htm'),('Catechism of the Catholic Church §§1163–1173, the liturgical year',CCC),('USCCB: Liturgical Year and Calendar',USCCB),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS),('Vanderbilt Divinity Library: Revised Common Lectionary',RCL),('Catholic Encyclopedia: Liturgical Colours',COL)]),
dict(slug='christmas',name='Christmas',color='#e9d9a6',colname='White and gold',weeks=2,icon='⭐',
dates='25 December to the Baptism of the Lord (Catholic; 10 January 2027) or to 5 January, the Twelve Days (Lutheran).',
history='''The earliest firm evidence for a feast of Christ's birth on 25 December is the Roman *Chronograph of 354*, whose list of martyrs (drawn up c. 336) begins: "VIII kal. Ian. natus Christus in Betleem Iudeae". The East kept 6 January for both birth and Epiphany, and gradually took up 25 December in the late 4th century; St John Chrysostom preached in Antioch in 386 that the December feast had reached them only a few years before. The date may come from calculation (the conception reckoned on 25 March, nine months earlier) as much as from any Roman festival (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Christmas").''',
why='''The Word became flesh (John 1:14). Christmas celebrates that God did not save the world from a distance but entered it as a child. The season holds a cluster of feasts that look at the Incarnation from different sides: St Stephen (the first martyr), St John the Evangelist, the Holy Innocents, the Holy Family, Mary Mother of God / the Name of Jesus (1 January).''',
symbols='''**Crib or crèche** (St Francis staged the first living one at Greccio in 1223), the **Christmas tree** (German and Lutheran custom popularised in the 16th–19th centuries), **candles and lights** (Christ the light of the world), **three Masses** on Christmas Day in the Roman tradition: at night, at dawn and during the day.''',
catholic='''Christmas is a solemnity with an octave; 1 January, the Solemnity of Mary, Mother of God, closes the octave and is a holy day of obligation. The season ends with the Baptism of the Lord. Midnight Mass is one of the most attended liturgies of the year.''',
lutheran='''Lutherans keep the Twelve Days to 5 January. The RCL appoints three sets of propers for Christmas Eve and Day, and 1 January is kept as the Name of Jesus. Luther's carol *From Heaven Above to Earth I Come* (*Vom Himmel hoch*, 1535) was written for his own family's Christmas Eve.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia: Christmas',NA+'03724b.htm'),('USCCB: Liturgical Year and Calendar',USCCB),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS),('Vanderbilt RCL',RCL)]),
dict(slug='epiphany',name='Epiphany',color='#3f7d4a',colname='White for the feast; green for the weeks after (Lutheran)',weeks=5,icon='✨',
dates='Feast on 6 January (in the US Catholic calendar, the Sunday between 2 and 8 January: 3 January 2027). Lutheran "Time after Epiphany" runs to Transfiguration Sunday (7 February 2027).',
history='''*Epiphaneia* is Greek for "manifestation". It is older than Christmas: Clement of Alexandria (c. 200) mentions Egyptian Christians keeping Christ's baptism on 6 January. In the East it became the great feast of the Baptism ("Theophany"); in the West, the coming of the Magi. Ancient Western texts join three manifestations: the star to the Magi, the voice at the Jordan, and the water made wine at Cana (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Epiphany").''',
why='''Christmas shows that Christ is born; Epiphany shows *who* he is and *for whom*. The Magi are the first Gentiles to worship him, so the feast is about the whole world being drawn to Christ, and the weeks after it read his first signs and calls.''',
symbols='''The **star**, the **three gifts** (gold for a king, frankincense for God, myrrh for one who will die), **chalking the door** (20+C+M+B+27, "Christus mansionem benedicat", May Christ bless this house), blessing of water in the East, King Cake and the Spanish *Día de Reyes*.''',
catholic='''Epiphany is a solemnity; the following Sunday is the Baptism of the Lord, and Ordinary Time begins the next day. On Epiphany the deacon may proclaim the dates of Easter and the movable feasts for the year, an old custom.''',
lutheran='''The ELCA and LCMS keep a "Time after Epiphany" with green vestments, framed by the Baptism of Our Lord and Transfiguration Sunday (the last Sunday before Lent), so the season moves from the river to the mountain.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia: Epiphany',NA+'05504c.htm'),('Clement of Alexandria, Stromata I.21 (New Advent)','https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/02101.htm'),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS),('Vanderbilt RCL',RCL)]),
dict(slug='lent',name='Lent',color='#4a2f6e',colname='Violet (unbleached linen or "Lenten array" in some churches)',weeks=6,icon='✝️',
dates='Ash Wednesday to Holy Thursday. 2027: 10 February to 25 March.',
history='''A fast before Easter is very ancient. The Council of Nicaea (325) already speaks of "the forty days" (*tessarakoste*), and St Athanasius's Festal Letters of the 330s urge the Egyptian Church to keep them. The number recalls Moses on Sinai, Elijah's journey to Horeb and above all Christ's forty days in the wilderness. Lent was also the final preparation of catechumens for baptism at Easter, which is why its Gospels (the Samaritan woman, the man born blind, Lazarus) are baptismal. Ashes on the first day were at first given to public penitents; by the 11th century everyone received them (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Lent").''',
why='''Lent is forty days of return: prayer, fasting and almsgiving (Matthew 6), to clear room for God and for the poor, and to walk with Christ toward his Passion. Pope St Leo the Great's Lenten sermons (5th century) describe it as a yearly renewal of the whole Church.''',
symbols='''**Ashes** ("Remember that you are dust", Genesis 3:19), the **purple** of penitence, the **Alleluia buried** until Easter, **veiled crosses** from the 5th Sunday, **Stations of the Cross**, rose vestments on Laetare (4th) Sunday, and the Lenten fish Friday.''',
catholic='''Ash Wednesday and Good Friday are days of fasting and abstinence for adults; Fridays of Lent are days of abstinence from meat. The Gloria and Alleluia are silent. RCIA candidates are enrolled and pass through the "Scrutinies" on the 3rd, 4th and 5th Sundays (with Year A readings).''',
lutheran='''Lutherans keep Ash Wednesday with imposition of ashes, midweek Lenten services (often working through the Passion or the Catechism), and fasting as a freely chosen discipline. Luther kept fasting as a good outward training while warning that it earns nothing before God.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia: Lent',NA+'09152a.htm'),('Athanasius, Festal Letters (New Advent)','https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2806.htm'),('Leo the Great, Sermons (New Advent)','https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3603.htm'),('USCCB: Liturgical Year and Calendar',USCCB),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS)]),
dict(slug='holy-week',name='Holy Week & Triduum',color='#9b1c1c',colname='Red (Palm Sunday, Good Friday), white (Holy Thursday, Easter Vigil)',weeks=1,icon='🌿',
dates='Palm Sunday to Holy Saturday. 2027: 21–27 March; the Triduum runs from Holy Thursday evening (25 March) to Easter Sunday evening.',
history='''The fullest early account comes from Egeria, a pilgrim (probably from Spain or Gaul) who kept Holy Week in Jerusalem around 381–384 and wrote it down for her sisters at home: a procession with palm and olive branches down the Mount of Olives, the night watch at Gethsemane, the veneration of the wood of the Cross on Friday, and the great Paschal vigil. Pilgrims carried these customs home and they shaped every later liturgy (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Holy Week").''',
why='''Holy Week walks day by day through Jesus' last days: his entry into Jerusalem, the Last Supper and washing of feet, his arrest, trial and crucifixion, and the silence of the tomb. The Triduum ("three days") is one celebration spread over three days, the high point of the whole year.''',
symbols='''**Palms**, the **washing of feet** (*mandatum*, whence "Maundy" Thursday), **stripping of the altar**, the **bare cross** venerated on Good Friday, the **new fire and Paschal candle** at the Easter Vigil, with the ancient *Exsultet*.''',
catholic='''Palm Sunday reads the whole Passion. On Holy Thursday morning the bishop blesses the holy oils at the Chrism Mass; in the evening the Mass of the Lord's Supper ends with the Eucharist carried to an altar of repose. Good Friday has no Mass: the Passion of John, the solemn intercessions, veneration of the Cross and Communion. The Easter Vigil is "the mother of all vigils" (St Augustine), with baptisms.''',
lutheran='''Lutherans keep the same shape: Sunday of the Passion, Maundy Thursday with confession and the Lord's Supper and the stripping of the altar, Good Friday (often Tenebrae, with candles put out one by one), and a growing return to the Easter Vigil. Bach's Passions were written for Good Friday Vespers in Leipzig.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia: Holy Week',NA+'07435a.htm'),('The Pilgrimage of Etheria (CCEL)','https://www.ccel.org/ccel/mcclure/etheria.html'),('Catechism §§1163–1173',CCC),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS)]),
dict(slug='easter',name='Easter',color='#f3e7b4',colname='White and gold',weeks=7,icon='🌅',
dates='Fifty days, Easter Sunday to Pentecost. 2027: 28 March to 16 May. Easter is the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after 21 March.',
history='''Easter is the oldest Christian feast, the Christian Pascha, kept from the beginning as the Passover of Christ. In the 2nd century a sharp dispute (the "Quartodeciman" controversy) asked whether to keep it on 14 Nisan or on the following Sunday; St Polycarp and Pope Anicetus disagreed in peace. The Council of Nicaea (325) settled on a Sunday after the spring full moon, independent of the Jewish calendar. The Eastern Orthodox still use the Julian calendar for the calculation, so their Easter often falls later (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Easter").''',
why='''"If Christ be not raised, your faith is vain" (1 Corinthians 15:17, KJV). The Resurrection is the heart of the Gospel, and the Church celebrates it for fifty days as one "great Sunday" (St Athanasius). The readings come from Acts, showing the Risen Lord's life in the first church.''',
symbols='''The **Paschal candle** lit from the new fire, the **Alleluia** restored, **lilies**, **eggs** (the sealed tomb broken open; once forbidden in Lent), **holy water** sprinkled in memory of baptism, the greeting *Christ is risen! He is risen indeed!*''',
catholic='''Easter Sunday is followed by an octave ending on Divine Mercy Sunday. Ascension falls forty days after Easter (Thursday 6 May 2027; moved to Sunday 9 May in most US dioceses). The Regina Caeli replaces the Angelus. Every Catholic is obliged to receive Communion during Easter time.''',
lutheran='''Lutherans call the Sundays "of Easter" (not "after Easter") to keep the fifty days as one feast. Ascension Day is kept on the Thursday or the following Sunday. Many congregations keep the old Easter Vigil and sunrise services.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia: Easter',NA+'05224d.htm'),('Athanasius, Festal Letters',  'https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2806.htm'),('USCCB: Liturgical Year and Calendar',USCCB),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS),('Vanderbilt RCL',RCL)]),
dict(slug='pentecost',name='Pentecost',color='#c4291c',colname='Red',weeks=1,icon='🔥',
dates='The fiftieth day of Easter. 2027: Sunday 16 May.',
history='''*Pentekoste* is Greek for "fiftieth". It was first the Jewish harvest Feast of Weeks (Shavuot), fifty days after Passover, which in later Jewish tradition also remembered the giving of the Law at Sinai. On that feast the Holy Spirit came on the apostles in wind and fire (Acts 2). Tertullian (c. 200) already names Pentecost as a solemn time for baptism; in England it was called **Whitsunday** from the white robes of the newly baptized (Catholic Encyclopedia, "Pentecost").''',
why='''Pentecost completes Easter: the Risen Christ pours out the Spirit, and the Church goes out to every nation. The crowd hearing one message in many languages reverses the scattering at Babel. It is often called the birthday of the Church.''',
symbols='''**Red** for fire, the **dove**, **tongues of flame**, wind, the sequence *Veni Sancte Spiritus* and the hymn *Veni Creator Spiritus* (9th century, attributed to Rabanus Maurus), red flowers, and readings in several languages.''',
catholic='''Pentecost is a solemnity closing the Easter season, often with a vigil Mass. The Paschal candle moves to the baptistery. Confirmations are common in this season.''',
lutheran='''Lutherans wear red and often celebrate Affirmation of Baptism (Confirmation). Luther's *Come, Holy Ghost, God and Lord* (*Komm, Heiliger Geist, Herre Gott*, 1524) expands an older Latin antiphon.''',
sources=[('Catholic Encyclopedia: Pentecost',NA+'15614b.htm'),('Acts 2 (KJV, public domain)','https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Acts+2&version=KJV'),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS),('Vanderbilt RCL',RCL)]),
dict(slug='ordinary-time',name='Ordinary Time / Season after Pentecost',color='#2f6b3a',colname='Green (white for feasts, red for martyrs)',weeks=26,icon='🌱',
dates='Catholic Ordinary Time: from the day after the Baptism of the Lord to Ash Wednesday, and from the day after Pentecost to Christ the King. Lutheran "Time after Pentecost": 17 May to 27 November 2027.',
history='''"Ordinary" comes from *ordinalis*, "numbered": these are the counted Sundays. Before 1969 the Roman calendar called them "Sundays after Epiphany" and "after Pentecost"; the reform after Vatican II joined them into one numbered season of 33 or 34 weeks. Lutherans and many Protestants keep the older names. Trinity Sunday (the first Sunday after Pentecost) spread through the West in the Middle Ages and was made universal by Pope John XXII in 1334. Christ the King, the last Sunday, was added by Pius XI in 1925.''',
why='''After the great feasts, the Church walks through Jesus' ministry Sunday by Sunday, reading one Gospel almost continuously (Matthew in Year A, Mark in Year B, Luke in Year C). Green is the colour of growth: this is where faith is lived out in ordinary weeks.''',
symbols='''**Green**, the numbered Sundays, harvest festivals, All Saints and All Souls in November, and Christ the King as the year's crown.''',
catholic='''Major celebrations inside the season: Trinity Sunday, Corpus Christi, the Sacred Heart, the Transfiguration (6 Aug), the Assumption (15 Aug), the Exaltation of the Cross (14 Sep), All Saints (1 Nov) and All Souls (2 Nov), and Christ the King.''',
lutheran='''The Lutheran Time after Pentecost includes Trinity Sunday, Reformation Day (31 October, often kept the Sunday before), All Saints' Sunday (when the names of members who died that year are read), and Christ the King. The LCMS still counts "Sundays after Pentecost".''',
sources=[('Catechism §§1163–1173',CCC),('USCCB: Liturgical Year and Calendar',USCCB),('LCMS: The Church Year',LCMS),('ELCA: Worship',ELCA),('Catholic Encyclopedia: All Saints',NA+'01315a.htm'),('Catholic Encyclopedia: All Souls',NA+'01315b.htm'),('Catholic Encyclopedia: Corpus Christi',NA+'04390b.htm')]),
]
